                <head>[Inclosure 2.—Translation.]</head>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Section III</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">of the Police Code</hi>).—<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Associations</hi>.</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Article</hi> 23.</p>
                <p>The police shall be obliged to protect every class of civil and religious
                    associations; but they shall prevent and dissolve those whose object is to
                    disturb the public peace or commit an infraction thereof, or if it is presumed
                    that the individuals who compose them will be armed or will cause a
                    conflict.</p>
                <p>Likewise the police shall be obliged to see that the exercise of every form of
                    worship and its ceremonies are respected, in conformity with the constitution of
                    the Republic.</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Article</hi> 24.</p>
                <p>Those who ridicule any religious act or the outward manifestations of any worship
                    shall be punished by a fine of from five to twenty-five sucres.</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Article</hi> 25.</p>
                <p>They shall be punished by a fine of from ten to one hundred sucres and
                    imprisonment for from three to thirty days— <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Who in any way assail the ceremonies of a worship which shall not have
                            been prohibited by the regulations of worship.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>Who interfere with the work of a minister of religion in the exercise
                            of his worship.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Who by violence, disorder, or scandal impede or disturb the exercise
                            of a form of worship.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>The ministers of a worship who, in their temples or religious places,
                            streets, or plazas, shall speak against the constitution or laws of the
                            Republic, or against an established political party, by instigating to
                            rebellion or dissatisfaction with the constituted authorities.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
            </div>
